WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest
is a talented actor, director,

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.570
and former
professional wrestler.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.239
He is creative director
of the Empire Circus,

00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:09.909
now in performances
through January 30th

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:11.877
at Empire Stores
in Dumbo, Brooklyn.

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:14.747
He also stars in
the new installment of "Scream,"

00:00:14.747 --> 00:00:16.850
which opens in theaters
January 14th.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.685
Please welcome to the show
David Arquette.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:21.187
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:31.197 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.865
How are you?
-I'm good. How are you?

00:00:32.865 --> 00:00:34.200
-Good.
It's wonderful to see you.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.635
And this is
a bit of coincidence.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:38.938
Benedict Cumberbatch
played Sherlock on the BBC.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.640
Quite famously.
-Amazing.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.909
And I actually played
Sherlock Holmes, too,

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:43.943
believe it or not.

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:45.077
-When did you play
Sherlock Holmes?

00:00:45.077 --> 00:00:49.082
-Onstage.
It was a comedic interpretation.

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.450
It was a bit of a stretch,
I must say.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.119
[ Laughter ]
-How did it go?

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:55.254
It was not received...?
-I had a full breakdown.

00:00:55.254 --> 00:00:56.989
We can't talk about it.
-Okay. We won't talk about it.

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:59.826
We'll move on.
-Oh! PTSD.

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:01.094
-Oh, I'm so sorry.
I didn't mean to bring it up.

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:02.829
-It's okay, no, no.
It's quite all right.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:05.164
I love him on that show.
-You, um --

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:06.899
This is very exciting that
there's a new installment

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:08.200
of "Scream."
-Yes.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.003
-And I imagine this is something
where it's very important

00:01:11.003 --> 00:01:13.706
not to let the plot
leak to the public

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.174
but also to the cast,
to some degree.

00:01:15.174 --> 00:01:16.475
Did they keep it secret
from you?

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:18.878
-Yeah.
There's different scripts

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.613
and different things they shoot.

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:23.983
Yeah, it's really --
it's a wonderful opportunity

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.652
to be part of something
for 25 years.

00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:28.187
I love playing
the role of Dewey.

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:31.291
He's such an iconic character.
-Dewey is awesome.

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:34.827
And I did not realize this.
So, Dewey was supposed to die

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:38.231
in the first "Scream,"
and, basically, you were just

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:40.333
too much of a hit.
-[ Laughing ]

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.468
-Well, Wes Craven
was mean enough to say,

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:44.771
"Well, David, we're gonna
put you on a gurney.

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:48.241
We'll roll you out, and we'll
see if you make it or not."

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:50.009
And then, the second time
we did it, he said,

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.946
"Okay. This time,
just give a little thumbs-up.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.348
I don't know
if I'll use it or not."

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:59.519
But thank God for Wes Craven,
'cause I got a baby out of it.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:01.954
Yeah, now, this is --
-I mean, not Wes Craven.

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:05.424
Thank Courteney Cox, but...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:07.794
-Yeah, that made it sound
like the cast gift

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:09.128
was that Wes Craven
gave everyone a baby.

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:10.196
[ Laughter ]
-No, no.

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:12.564
-So, you met your ex,
Courteney Cox,

00:02:12.564 --> 00:02:14.133
on the set of "Scream."
-Yes.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:17.503
-And you guys had a daughter.
-I'm sorry if that was weird.

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:18.904
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.904 --> 00:02:21.707
-Yes, we have a daughter, Coco.
She's 17 years old now.

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:23.743
-So that must be nice,
'cause you can always remember

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:26.445
exactly how long ago
"Scream" was because you just

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:27.814
look at your daughter
and you work backwards, right?

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.115
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:31.250
-Yeah, it was a few "Screams"
in there, but, yeah.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.284
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.284 --> 00:02:33.719
-Oh, it wasn't
the first "Scream."

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:36.021
-No, the first "Scream,"
we were falling in love.

00:02:36.021 --> 00:02:37.790
The second "Scream,"
she hated me.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.526
[ Laughter ]
I think by the third "scream,"

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:42.127
we -- we had --
-That's so good that there

00:02:42.127 --> 00:02:44.230
were sequels, or else
there would be no Coco.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.698
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:48.534
Wes craven sadly passed away
in 2015,

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:51.304
but I was lucky enough
to meet people who knew him

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.105
and loved him, and he just --
It's just funny,

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:56.409
because you associate his name
with horror and terror,

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:58.444
but it sounds like he was just
the sweetest man in the world.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.413
-Yeah, he was
an avid birdwatcher,

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:02.081
which most people don't know.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.816
But he was
this incredible professor,

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:06.786
and he's just so soft-spoken
and had a wry sense of humor.

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:10.456
He'd say things like,
"David, that was unusable."

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:13.226
[ Laughter ]
I was like, "Okay, Wes.

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:15.294
Well, you got any direction
for me?

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:16.662
What -- What do --"

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:19.398
"Do it again, do it faster,
and do it better."

00:03:19.398 --> 00:03:21.667
[ Laughter ]
-Very humble, very humble note.

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.269
-I miss him.
I love you, Wes.

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.704
-This is very exciting,
because I feel like

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:28.975
not a lot of people think
that circuses are coming back,

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:30.943
but yet you are
physically doing that.

00:03:30.943 --> 00:03:32.278
You are bringing
the circus back.

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:33.879
-Yes, we're bringing
the circus back.

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:37.950
-So, how did this happen?
-Oh, Randy Weiner is an amazing,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.520
uh, I don't know
how you'd say it.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.222
Like, place thrower?
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:45.625
He works with a company
called Remarkable Entertainment,

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:47.826
and he did "Sleep No More"
in New York,

00:03:47.826 --> 00:03:49.895
so he does these
incredible venues.

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:52.398
And this is --
yeah, and this is going to be

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.067
an interactive circus
like you've never seen.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:57.736
You know, circuses
need to, like, come back.

00:03:57.736 --> 00:04:00.907
We need joy and happiness,
and, you know,

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:02.341
and that's what it's about.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:04.744
I recently bought the rights
to Bozo the Clown.

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.212
-So, that is amazing to me...
-Yeah.

00:04:07.212 --> 00:04:10.917
-...that the rights to
Bozo the Clown were available.

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:12.084
-Yeah.
It's really hard.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:14.319
It took me 15 years
to close the deal.

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:16.455
I'm not kidding.
It was hard.

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:18.524
-So, you own --
You are the man who owns --

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:20.726
-I own Bozo the Clown!
-Congratulations.

00:04:20.726 --> 00:04:22.028
That's a big deal.
-Yes.

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:24.864
-And clowns
need rehabilitation right now,

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.699
you know, especially in America.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.601
The scary clown has, like,
taken over.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.236
-That's true.
It was not be helpful.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.904
-I just want them
to go back to Halloween.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:33.138
Yes, it was not helpful.

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:35.574
-Your dad dressed up
like a clown a lot?

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:38.444
-Yeah. My dad dressed up
as Butter the Clown.

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:40.846
And he would come
to my birthday parties,

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:44.116
and then he'd leave,
and then my dad would show up.

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:45.218
I said, "Dad,
you just missed Butter!"

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:47.119
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:49.155
-So, he would actually
trick you into thinking

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:50.856
that a different person
had showed up.

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:54.160
-Yeah, one time,
he got an opportunity

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.996
to get a free photograph
at a bank

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:58.731
if he opened a bank account.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:01.734
And he made us
all dress up as clowns,

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:04.036
and he dressed up
as Butter the Clown.

00:05:04.036 --> 00:05:06.238
-So, is the bank photo?
-I should've known at this point

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:07.740
that, you know --
-Is this the bank photo?

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.241
-Yeah, that's the bank photo!
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.241 --> 00:05:10.809
-That's really funny.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.809 --> 00:05:13.913
-I should note, I think,
is that Patricia Arquette?

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:15.848
That's your sister Patricia,
Rosanna Arquette?

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:17.850
-No, no,
that's Richmond Arquette,

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.252
and that's Alexis.
-Oh, that's Alexis.

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:22.522
-And my mom.
-That's fantastic.

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:24.724
Very nice.
-Yeah, Rosanna had already

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:27.193
moved out to Hollywood
to start her career.

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:28.994
-That's right.
She didn't want to come back

00:05:28.994 --> 00:05:30.095
for the clown picture?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:32.498
-No, she didn't.
No, she didn't.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:35.501
-And is this -- is this
you clowning it up right here?

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.237
-Yeah.
So that's me in Butter's vest,

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:41.841
and, yeah.
So I'm learning how to clown

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:44.944
with my creative director
partner, Gabe Dell,

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:48.347
who was a former
Cirque du Soleil clown.

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:50.817
So we want to bring
joy and happiness back.

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:52.284
-And it's interesting
that you say that you're

00:05:52.284 --> 00:05:54.686
learning how to do it,
because clowning is a very --

00:05:54.686 --> 00:05:56.321
You know, professional clowns
take that very seriously,

00:05:56.321 --> 00:05:57.456
and there's a way
to do it right,

00:05:57.456 --> 00:05:58.624
and there's a way
to do it wrong.

00:05:58.624 --> 00:05:59.892
Do you feel like you're
naturally inclined

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:02.461
to be a good clown?
-You tell me.

00:06:02.461 --> 00:06:03.863
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:05.130
-I think you're gonna
be all right.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.365
I think you're gonna
be all right.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:07.700
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.036
-I mean, I have a clown teacher.
There's teachers --

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:14.373
Misha Usov is this incredible
Cirque clown, as well,

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:17.409
and he's my teacher, so you
learn certain clown stuff.

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:20.446
-Now, is clown school
or clown instruction,

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:23.782
is it serious or is it --
-Oh, of course it's serious.

00:06:23.782 --> 00:06:26.519
Are you kidding?
I'm serious!

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.387
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.387 --> 00:06:31.157
-Very good. Very good.
I mean, you're a natural.

00:06:31.157 --> 00:06:32.925
[ Both laugh ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:36.362
Now, it should be noted
that, before clowning,

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:37.796
before you
started your clowning,

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:39.498
you were
a professional wrestler.

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.067
-Yeah.
-So, this is sort of recent.

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:45.104
-Wrestling's a lot like
clowning, to be honest with you.

00:06:45.104 --> 00:06:47.039
There's a lot of laughs,
but it hurts.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:48.373
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:48.373 --> 00:06:51.043
-I would imagine clowning
hurts less than wrestling did.

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:54.179
-Well, that just --
it depends on what your act is.

00:06:54.179 --> 00:06:56.749
There's a lot of painful --
clowning can be --

00:06:56.749 --> 00:07:00.852
There's stunts, and there's --
They're so -- There's so many

00:07:00.852 --> 00:07:03.456
beautiful clowns that
the world doesn't know about.

00:07:03.456 --> 00:07:07.726
So I really just --
You know, Bozo used to say,

00:07:07.726 --> 00:07:11.097
"It's nice to be important, but
it's more important to be nice."

00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:13.299
-Oh, that's very good.
-And we want to make

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:15.835
kindness cool again.
-That's really -- all right.

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:18.170
-Well, we're gonna leave it
to Bozo for that.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:21.640
-All you Bozos!
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.043
-Now, your professional wrestl--
This is your --

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:26.145
Will you tell everybody
the name --

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:28.380
your professional wrestling name
before I show the photo?

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.051
-Well, this was a specific one,
but I'm a certified

00:07:32.051 --> 00:07:34.086
Bob Ross instructor so...
-Okay.

00:07:34.086 --> 00:07:36.154
-...I can teach you how to paint
like Bob Ross.

00:07:36.154 --> 00:07:38.424
-Now, who certifies you
for that?

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:40.226
You have so many --
Your teachers

00:07:40.226 --> 00:07:41.327
are the weirdest teachers
in the world.

00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:43.295
-Yeah, I know.
-Like, the Bob Ross guy

00:07:43.295 --> 00:07:44.864
shows up,
and the clown guy leaves.

00:07:44.864 --> 00:07:46.398
-You go
to New Smyrna Beach, Florida.

00:07:46.398 --> 00:07:48.867
It's a three-week course.
You can all do it.

00:07:48.867 --> 00:07:51.404
-So you went to Florida
to become a certified

00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:52.605
Bob Ross instructor.
-Yes.

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:54.173
-Okay. And then,
you used that to create --

00:07:54.173 --> 00:07:57.109
-No, no, no.
I wrestled as Rob Boss.

00:07:57.109 --> 00:07:59.478
-Okay.
Wrestling, pro wrestling.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:01.848
-Okay. So you were --
Now, it's very funny.

00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:03.583
When I think of wrestlers
and I think of

00:08:03.583 --> 00:08:05.717
wrestling characters,
I would never think,

00:08:05.717 --> 00:08:08.954
"Oh, a Bob Ross wrestler
would be what people

00:08:08.954 --> 00:08:10.522
would want to see.
-Rob Boss.

00:08:10.522 --> 00:08:12.992
-Rob Boss -- I'm sorry.
-I think we're tiptoeing

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:15.660
around an I.P. right now.
-It's very clear.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.831
You own Bozo but not Bob Ross.
That's coming across.

00:08:18.831 --> 00:08:21.967
-Rob Boss is who I wrestled as.
-Here's --

00:08:21.967 --> 00:08:23.669
Now, I'm gonna be honest.
I'm not a professional --

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:28.106
-But I used the official
Bob Ross materials.

00:08:28.106 --> 00:08:30.008
-I'm not a professional
wrestler, but I would not be

00:08:30.008 --> 00:08:31.810
the most intimidated
I've ever been

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:35.548
if I stepped into the ring
with Rob Boss.

00:08:35.548 --> 00:08:36.782
[ Laughter ]

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:39.685
-Well, he didn't put you
in a sharpshooter, did he?

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:42.354
-I don't know. I don't know
what any of these words mean!

00:08:42.354 --> 00:08:44.823
-I was supposed
to get RJ City on a talk show,

00:08:44.823 --> 00:08:46.058
and I haven't done it.

00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:48.727
That's why he tag-teamed
with me, so I'm sorry, RJ.

00:08:48.727 --> 00:08:50.395
-All right, RJ next time.

00:08:50.395 --> 00:08:51.464
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:53.065
You guys, that's David Arquette.

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:56.035
Empire Circus performances
run through January 30th,

00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:58.738
And "Scream" opens in theaters
January 14th.

